Thus almost everything needs to be achieved. However many of these are interdependent.

We need to construct a plan that builds up shift_candidate_beta similarly to shift_candidate_alpha (which already has many of these properties). Indeed shift_candidate_alpha already has many of the needed predicates: candidate_prepared, candidate_finalized, dispatch_allocated, dispatch_ready, dispatched, candidate_vehicle_linked (to vehicle_unit_gamma), candidate_loading_container_linked (to loading_container_alpha), candidate_origin_availability_linked, candidate_destination_availability_linked, candidate_compliance_certificate_linked, compliance_validation_complete, constraint_applied, operator_alignment_confirmed, candidate_dispatch_slot_linked (to dispatch_slot_alpha), etc. Also we have transport_leg_available etc.

Thus we can mimic the steps used for shift_candidate_alpha but for shift_candidate_beta, using the available resources.

But note: some resources are already used by shift_candidate_alpha: e.g., dispatch_slot_alpha is already linked to shift_candidate_alpha (candidate_dispatch_slot_linked shift_candidate_alpha dispatch_slot_alpha). However the goal also requires dispatch_slot_alpha linked to destination_availability_alpha, origin_availability_alpha, and shift_candidate_beta. The dispatch slot can be linked to multiple candidates? The predicate candidate_dispatch_slot_linked is not functional; we can link multiple candidates to same dispatch slot. The allocate_dispatch_slot_for_candidate action requires dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot as precondition, and then removes dispatch_slot_available. So after linking to one candidate, the dispatch slot becomes unavailable (not available). So we cannot link multiple candidates if we follow that action, because after first allocation, dispatch_slot_available becomes false, preventing further allocations. However the goal requires dispatch_slot_alpha to be linked to three candidates (destination, origin, shift_candidate_beta). This seems impossible under the action's effects unless we can release the dispatch slot and reallocate? There is no action to release dispatch slot. The only action that affects dispatch_slot_available is allocate_dispatch_slot_for_candidate (removes availability) and execute_origin_dispatch/execute_destination_dispatch/execute_schedule_dispatch (adds dispatch_slot_available). So after we execute a dispatch, the dispatch slot becomes available again. So we could allocate dispatch slot to a candidate, then execute dispatch (which frees the slot), then allocate again to another candidate, etc. That would allow linking multiple candidates sequentially, but at any given time only one candidate would have dispatch_allocated and candidate_dispatch_slot_linked? Actually after dispatch, the effect adds dispatch_slot_available and removes dispatched? Wait execute_origin_dispatch effect: (dispatched ?origin_availability) (vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit) (dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot). It does NOT remove dispatch_allocated or candidate_dispatch_slot_linked. So after dispatch, we still have dispatch_allocated true and candidate_dispatch_slot_linked true, and also dispatch_slot_available becomes true. So we would have both dispatch_allocated and dispatch_slot_available true. That's allowed. Then we could allocate another candidate? The precondition for allocate_dispatch_slot_for_candidate includes (not (dispatch_allocated ?candidate)) and (dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot). It does not require that the dispatch slot is not already linked to another candidate. So we could allocate another candidate while the slot is still allocated to the first candidate, as long as dispatch_slot_available is true (which it is after dispatch). However we also need dispatch_allocated ?candidate to be false for the new candidate (which holds). So we could allocate dispatch slot to multiple candidates sequentially, each time after a dispatch makes the slot available again. However the allocate action does not check that the slot is not already linked to another candidate; it just adds candidate_dispatch_slot_linked. So we could have multiple links.

Thus we can achieve multiple dispatch_slot_links by doing: allocate dispatch slot for candidate1, execute dispatch (makes slot available), allocate for candidate2, execute dispatch, allocate for candidate3, execute dispatch. At the end, all three candidates will have dispatch_allocated true and candidate_dispatch_slot_linked true, and dispatch_slot_available will be true after the last dispatch. Goal requires dispatch_slot_available? Not in goal. Actually goal does not mention dispatch_slot_available; it's not required. So final state can have dispatch_slot_available true or false; doesn't matter.

Thus we can proceed.

Now we need to build up shift_candidate_beta through similar steps as shift_candidate_alpha.

Let's outline the steps needed for a schedule candidate (like shift_candidate_beta) to reach goal:

We need:

- candidate_created (already true)
- candidate_prepared (need to do confirm_candidate_preparedness? Actually candidate_prepared is a predicate that can be set via confirm_candidate_preparedness action. Precondition: candidate_created ?candidate, vehicle_allocated ?candidate, candidate_task_profile_linked ?candidate ?route_task_profile, (not (candidate_prepared ?candidate)). Effect: candidate_prepared ?candidate.

Thus to get candidate_prepared shift_candidate_beta, we need vehicle_allocated shift_candidate_beta and candidate_task_profile_linked shift_candidate_beta route_task_profile_alpha.

- vehicle_allocated shift_candidate_beta: need allocate_vehicle_unit action. Precondition: candidate_created ?candidate, (not (vehicle_allocated ?candidate)), vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit. Effect: vehicle_allocated ?candidate, candidate_vehicle_linked ?candidate ?vehicle_unit, (not vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit).

We have vehicle_unit_gamma available (vehicle_available vehicle_unit_gamma). However note that shift_candidate_alpha already has candidate_vehicle_linked shift_candidate_alpha vehicle_unit_gamma and vehicle_allocated shift_candidate_alpha. But allocate action only checks vehicle_allocated ?candidate false and vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit true. It does not check that the vehicle is not already linked to another candidate. So we can allocate vehicle_unit_gamma to shift_candidate_beta as well. That will add another candidate_vehicle_linked and keep the existing one. So after allocation, vehicle_allocated shift_candidate_beta true, candidate_vehicle_linked shift_candidate_beta vehicle_unit_gamma true, and vehicle_available vehicle_unit_gamma becomes false (since we remove it). However we also have vehicle_available vehicle_unit_gamma in init; after allocation it will be false. That's fine; goal does not require vehicle_available vehicle_unit_gamma.

